<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cécile Fatiman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1845">1845</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cécil Attiman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cécile Fatiman was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou</a> priestess (manbo) and revolutionary who, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutty-boukman">Dutty Boukman</a>, led the ceremony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bois-caiman">Bois Caïman</a> that marked the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>. The daughter of an enslaved African woman and a Corsican prince, she and her mother were bought and sold as slaves, and she gained her freedom before or during the rising of 1791. On the night of 21–22 August 1791 she presided over the ceremony, which gathered 200 enslaved people: a black pig was sacrificed, and those present swore to kill the white slavers and to obey Boukman as their commander. She later married the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-louis-pierrot">Jean-Louis Pierrot</a> and reportedly died at the age of 112.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The priestess <b>Cécile Fatiman</b> presided with Boukman over the ceremony at Bois Caïman in August 1791; Micaela Bastidas commanded rebels beside her husband Túpac Amaru II and was executed in Cusco in 1781; and in 1900 Yaa Asantewaa led the last armed resistance to British rule in Asante.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>In August 1791 the priestess <b>Cécile Fatiman</b> presided with Boukman over the ceremony at Bois Caïman.</blockquote><a href="/societies/saint-domingue">Social Hierarchy of Saint-Domingue</a></li></ul></section>
